TraceIQ Project Architecture & Assessment
As your technical co-founder, I have analyzed the entire TraceIQ codebase, including the infrastructure, backend API, frontend application, and the Node.js execution engine.

Here is a breakdown of the current state, trade-offs, and strategic recommendations for scaling to a production-grade SaaS.

1. System Architecture Overview
The system is a modern, distributed, microservices-oriented architecture fully containerized via Docker.

1.1 Infrastructure
PostgreSQL 15: Primary relational data store.
Redis 7: Used as a message broker for Celery, execution job queuing (Redis Streams), and result aggregation.
MinIO: S3-compatible object storage for storing test artifacts (videos, traces, screenshots).
Docker Compose: Well-structured definitions with multiple profiles (dev, prod, distributed) offering scalable capabilities for execution workers.
1.2 Backend (Python / FastAPI)
Framework: FastAPI with Uvicorn.
ORM: SQLModel + SQLAlchemy with AsyncPG for asynchronous database access.
Asynchronous Processing: Celery workers handle background tasks, test execution scheduling, and result aggregation.
Data Model: Implements a robust multi-tenant hierarchy (
Tenant
 -> 
Workspace
 -> 
Project
 -> 
TestSuite
 -> 
TestCase
) complete with granular RBAC (
Role
, 
Permission
, 
UserSystemRole
, 
Team
).
1.3 Execution Engine (Node.js / Playwright)
Framework: Express server & Redis Stream consumers (workers).
Playwright: Handles actual browser automation (Chromium, Firefox, WebKit).
Execution Modes: Supports continuous (shared browser context) and parallel execution.
Artifact Management: Records video, captures network logs, handles traces, and automatically uploads these to MinIO.
Communication: Posts progress and final results back to the backend via webhook or Redis queue.
1.4 Frontend (React / Vite)
Framework: React 19 + Vite (Not Next.js, as initially outlined, but a fast SPA).
Styling: Tailwind CSS + Radix UI primitives for accessible, customizable components.
State Management: React Query (TanStack Query) for server state and Axios for API requests. React Hook Form for form validations.
Routing: React Router DOM.
2. Strengths & Good Practices
True Multi-Tenancy & RBAC: The data model deeply embeds multi-tenancy (
Tenant
 and 
Workspace
) and fine-grained access logs (
AuditLog
), which is critical for B2B SaaS.
Distributed Test Execution Strategy: Utilizing Redis streams to distribute Playwright execution across scalable NodeJS worker nodes (execution-worker) prevents the Python backend from becoming a bottleneck.
Artifact Isolation: Using MinIO ensures that heavy binaries (videos/traces) do not bloat the database and can be migrated directly to AWS S3 in production.
Resilient Playwright Contexts: The runner manages contexts defensively, capturing traces and videos natively.
3. Areas for Improvement & Recommendations
To elevate this to a "production-quality, long-lived SaaS platform", I recommend focusing on the following areas:

3.1 Backend & Architecture
API Versioning: Currently, routes are prefixed with /api. We should introduce /api/v1 to allow backward compatibility as the SaaS evolves.
Database Migrations: Alembic is present. We need to ensure zero-downtime migration strategies are documented and enforced, especially around tenant data.
Rate Limiting & Throttling: Add Redis-backed rate limiting to the FastAPI application to prevent noisy tenants from exhausting API resources or execution queues.
Soft Deletes: Implementing soft-deletes (e.g., deleted_at) across core models (
Project
, 
TestSuite
, 
TestCase
) is highly recommended for SaaS platforms to allow data recovery.
3.2 Frontend & UI/UX
Vite vs. Next.js: The prompt mentioned Next.js, but the frontend is currently a React Vite SPA. If SEO for public pages or Server-Side Rendering (SSR) for initial load performance is a priority, we should consider migrating to Next.js. If it remains an authenticated SPA dashboard, Vite is perfectly fine, but requires careful bundle splitting.
SaaS Aesthetics: The code utilizes Tailwind + Radix UI ( likely shadcn/ui). We must ensure a highly polished, "Wow" factor design—using subtle glassmorphism, smooth Framer Motion micro-interactions, dark mode support, and empty states.
Real-time Updates: The backend exposes websockets. We should ensure the frontend deeply integrates these for live test execution streaming (seeing steps execute in real-time).
3.3 Execution Engine
Sandboxing & Security: Currently, Playwright runs user-defined steps. We must strictly sanitize any dynamic JS evaluation (e.g., page.evaluate()) to prevent malicious tenants from accessing the host environment or MinIO secrets.
Browser Context Leaks: Ensure that parallel execution cleans up browser contexts deterministically. Zombie Chromium processes will consume all host RAM quickly.
